==KTK Chimes==
[[File:KTK chimes.mp3|The KTK chimes.]]

The KTK chimes have been in use since it was first introduced with the company in 1924; as television networks were emerging, Hiroshi Tanaka, an engineer working for the Okami Broadcasting Company, recognized the need for a distinct auditory signal for program transitions. Tanaka, skilled in both music and engineering, conducted experiments late into the night at the station's sound studio. His objective was to devise a non-disruptive sonic brand for the station. After weeks of trial and error, Tanaka, with station management support, finalized a melodic sequence of chimes — 12 distinct notes with a musical quality. The chimes, implemented by the Okami Broadcasting Corporation, quickly gained recognition, becoming synonymous with the channel.

Inspired by the station's success, other Sanese  television stations adopted their own broadcasting chimes. Over the years, the use of broadcasting chimes proliferated across the industry, with each station striving to create a distinctive sound reflecting its identity.
With the rise of color television in the 1960s and the expansion of cable channels, the importance of broadcasting chimes grew. They became a familiar auditory cue connecting viewers to their preferred programs. In the digital era, as streaming platforms transformed the media landscape, traditional broadcasting chimes faced challenges. Nevertheless, many streaming services incorporated virtual chimes into their interfaces as a nod to the historical significance of these auditory signals in Sanese television.

